## The eight-unit concentration

In 2025, projects with exactly eight units dominated the fiveplex to eightplex category in the RS Zone, a direct consequence of the eight-unit cap. @tbl-unit-breakdown covers all RS Zone permits from 1 to 8 units, comparing Q1–Q3 2025 to Q1–Q3 2026. The eight-unit rowhome is still the single biggest source of small-scale homes, essentially flat from last year at 180 permits and 1,440 homes (184 and 1,472 in Q1–Q3 2025). What's new this quarter is growth just below the cap: seven-unit permits jumped from 15 to 50, and six-unit permits rose from 31 to 47. As a result, eight-unit projects make up 49% of RS Zone homes this year, down from 56% last year.

Altogether, RS Zone permits for 1 to 8 units added 2,925 homes in Q1–Q3 2026, up from 2,622 last year, even as the city as a whole permitted fewer homes. Given that eight-unit projects continue to account for the largest share of small-scale supply, I remain glad that City Council chose not to reduce the number of units permitted to six. The next section puts numbers on what a lower cap would cost, should the provincial government interfere.


